WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.338 --> 00:00:05.834
In the last lesson,
we covered the basics

2
00:00:05.834 --> 00:00:08.330
of semantic search and
saw that it worked

3
00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:11.138
pretty well for a
good amount of use cases.

4
00:00:11.138 --> 00:00:14.258
But we also saw some
edge cases and saw how

5
00:00:14.258 --> 00:00:18.627
things could go a little bit wrong.
In this lesson, we're going to deep

6
00:00:18.627 --> 00:00:22.371
dive on retrieval and cover a
few more advanced methods for overcoming

7
00:00:22.371 --> 00:00:23.307
those edge cases.

8
00:00:24.408 --> 00:00:27.025
I'm really excited by this
because I think retrieval

9
00:00:27.025 --> 00:00:30.514
is something that is
new and a lot of the techniques we're

10
00:00:30.514 --> 00:00:34.294
talking about have popped up
in the past few months or so. And

11
00:00:34.294 --> 00:00:36.911
this is a cutting edge
topic and so you

12
00:00:36.911 --> 00:00:40.401
guys are going to be right at the
forefront. Let's have some

13
00:00:40.401 --> 00:00:40.692
fun.

14
00:00:40.712 --> 00:00:43.683
In this lesson, we're
going to talk about retrieval. This

15
00:00:43.683 --> 00:00:47.545
is important at query time, when
you've got a query that comes in

16
00:00:47.545 --> 00:00:50.516
and you want to retrieved
the most relevant splits. We

17
00:00:50.516 --> 00:00:52.596
talked in the previous
lesson about semantic

18
00:00:52.596 --> 00:00:55.567
similarity search, but we're
going to talk about a few

19
00:00:55.567 --> 00:00:58.241
different and more advanced
methods here. The first one

20
00:00:58.241 --> 00:01:01.806
we're going to cover is Maximum Marginal Relevance,
or MMR. And so

21
00:01:01.806 --> 00:01:04.777
the idea behind this is that
if you just always

22
00:01:04.777 --> 00:01:07.154
take the documents that
are most similar to

23
00:01:07.154 --> 00:01:10.125
the query in the
embedding space, you may actually miss

24
00:01:10.125 --> 00:01:12.502
out on diverse information,
as we saw in

25
00:01:12.502 --> 00:01:17.553
one of the edge cases. In this example, we've
got a chef asking about all white mushrooms.

26
00:01:17.553 --> 00:01:20.821
And so if we take
a look at the most similar

27
00:01:20.821 --> 00:01:24.683
results, those would be the first
two documents, where they have a lot

28
00:01:24.683 --> 00:01:27.357
of information similar to the query
about a fruiting

29
00:01:27.357 --> 00:01:30.328
body and being all white. But
we really want to

30
00:01:30.328 --> 00:01:33.893
make sure that we
also get other information, like the fact that

31
00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:36.567
it's really poisonous. And so this
is where using

32
00:01:36.567 --> 00:01:39.538
MMR comes into play, as
it will select for a

33
00:01:39.538 --> 00:01:40.727
diverse set of documents.


34
00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:46.689
The idea behind MMR is
that we send a query in,

35
00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:50.398
and then we initially get
back a set of responses, with

36
00:01:50.398 --> 00:01:53.769
"fetch_k" being a parameter that we
can control in order

37
00:01:53.769 --> 00:01:57.478
to determine how many
responses we get. This is based solely

38
00:01:57.478 --> 00:02:00.849
on semantic similarity. From
there, we then work with that

39
00:02:00.849 --> 00:02:03.547
smaller set of documents
and optimize for not

40
00:02:03.547 --> 00:02:06.918
only the most relevant ones, based
on semantic similarity, but

41
00:02:06.918 --> 00:02:10.964
also ones that are diverse. And
from that set of documents, we

42
00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:13.998
choose a final "k" to
return to the user.

43
00:02:14.741 --> 00:02:18.898
Another type of retrieval we can
do is what we call self-query. So

44
00:02:18.898 --> 00:02:22.095
this is useful when you
get questions that aren't solely

45
00:02:22.095 --> 00:02:24.652
about the content that
you want to look

46
00:02:24.652 --> 00:02:27.530
up semantically, but also
include some mention of some

47
00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:31.686
metadata that you want to
do a filter on. So let's take the

48
00:02:31.686 --> 00:02:34.244
question, what are some
movies about aliens made

49
00:02:34.244 --> 00:02:38.080
in 1980? This really has
two components to it. It's got a

50
00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:40.958
semantic part, the aliens bit. So
we want to

51
00:02:40.958 --> 00:02:44.794
look up aliens in our database
of movies. But it's also got

52
00:02:44.794 --> 00:02:47.991
a piece that really refers to
the metadata about each

53
00:02:47.991 --> 00:02:51.508
movie, which is the fact that
the year should be 1980.

54
00:02:51.508 --> 00:02:55.664
What we can do is
we can use a language model itself to

55
00:02:55.664 --> 00:02:58.222
split that original question
into two separate things,

56
00:02:58.222 --> 00:03:01.739
a filter and a search
term. Most vector stores support a

57
00:03:01.739 --> 00:03:03.977
metadata filter. So
you can easily filter

58
00:03:03.977 --> 00:03:06.854
records based on metadata,
like the year being 1980.

59
00:03:06.854 --> 00:03:06.854


60
00:03:10.196 --> 00:03:12.350
Finally, we'll
talk about compression. This

61
00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:15.223
can be useful to
really pull out only

62
00:03:15.223 --> 00:03:18.454
the most relevant bits
of the retrieved passages. For

63
00:03:18.454 --> 00:03:22.044
example, when asking a question, you
get back the whole

64
00:03:22.044 --> 00:03:26.712
document that was stored, even
if only the first one or two sentences

65
00:03:26.712 --> 00:03:30.302
are the relevant parts. With
compression, you can then run

66
00:03:30.302 --> 00:03:34.611
all those documents through a language
model and extract the most relevant

67
00:03:34.611 --> 00:03:37.842
segments and then
pass only the most relevant segments

68
00:03:37.842 --> 00:03:40.355
into a final
language model call. This

69
00:03:40.355 --> 00:03:43.228
comes at the cost
of making more calls

70
00:03:43.228 --> 00:03:47.895
to the language model, but it's also
really good for focusing the final

71
00:03:47.895 --> 00:03:52.204
answer on only the most important
things. And so it's a bit

72
00:03:52.204 --> 00:03:56.871
of a tradeoff. Let's see these different
techniques in action. We're going to

73
00:03:56.871 --> 00:04:00.462
start by loading the environment variables
as we always do.

74
00:04:00.462 --> 00:04:00.462


75
00:04:00.462 --> 00:04:05.391
Let's then import Chroma and OpenAI
as we used them before. And

76
00:04:05.391 --> 00:04:10.319
we can see by looking at
the collection count that it's got

77
00:04:10.319 --> 00:04:14.838
the 209 documents that we
loaded in before. Let's now go

78
00:04:14.838 --> 00:04:18.945
over the example of max
marginal relevance. And so we'll

79
00:04:18.945 --> 00:04:25.106
load in the text from the example where we
have the information about mushrooms. For

80
00:04:25.106 --> 00:04:27.981
this example, we'll
create a small database

81
00:04:27.981 --> 00:04:32.910
that we can just
use as a toy example. We've got our

82
00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:36.607
question, and now we can run a
similarity search.

83
00:04:42.926 --> 00:04:46.871
We'll set "k=2" to only return the two most
relevant documents. And we

84
00:04:46.871 --> 00:04:49.906
can see that there is
no mention of the fact

85
00:04:49.906 --> 00:04:51.120
that it is poisonous.

86
00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:58.559
Let's now run it with MMR. While it's passing
"k=2", we still want

87
00:04:58.559 --> 00:05:06.570
to return two documents, but let's set
"fetch_k=3", where we fetch all three documents

88
00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:07.142
originally.


89
00:05:07.142 --> 00:05:10.730
We can now see that the
information that is

90
00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:13.122
poisonous is returned
among the documents

91
00:05:13.122 --> 00:05:14.318
that we retrieved.

92
00:05:14.318 --> 00:05:17.226
Let's go back to one
of the examples from

93
00:05:17.226 --> 00:05:19.812
the previous lesson when
we asked about MATLAB

94
00:05:19.812 --> 00:05:22.397
and got back documents
that had repeated information.

95
00:05:22.397 --> 00:05:25.629
To refresh your memory, we
can take a look at

96
00:05:25.629 --> 00:05:28.538
the first two documents, just
looking at the first

97
00:05:28.538 --> 00:05:31.446
few characters because they're pretty
long otherwise, and we

98
00:05:31.446 --> 00:05:34.355
can see that they're the same.
When we run

99
00:05:34.355 --> 00:05:39.203
MMR on these results, we can see
that the first one is the same as

100
00:05:39.203 --> 00:05:41.142
before because that's the most similar.


101
00:05:42.238 --> 00:05:45.933
But when we go on to the second one,
we can see that it's different. It's getting

102
00:05:45.933 --> 00:05:47.020
some diversity in the responses.


103
00:05:50.664 --> 00:05:54.146
Let's now move on to the self-query example.
This is the one where we

104
00:05:54.146 --> 00:05:56.633
had the question, what did
they say about regression in

105
00:05:56.633 --> 00:05:57.379
the third lecture?

106
00:05:58.541 --> 00:06:01.476
And it returned results from
not just the third lecture,

107
00:06:01.476 --> 00:06:05.585
but also the first and the second.
If we were fixing this by hand,

108
00:06:05.585 --> 00:06:08.519
what we would do is we'd
specify a metadata filter.

109
00:06:09.338 --> 00:06:12.498
So we'd pass in this information
that we want the

110
00:06:12.498 --> 00:06:15.342
source to be equal to the third lecture
PDF.

111
00:06:17.558 --> 00:06:19.555
And then if we
look at the documents

112
00:06:19.555 --> 00:06:22.300
that would be retrieved, they'd
all be from exactly that lecture.

113
00:06:24.859 --> 00:06:27.867
We can use a language
model to do this for us, so we don't

114
00:06:27.867 --> 00:06:28.942
have to manually specify that.

115
00:06:30.058 --> 00:06:33.290
To do this, we'll
import a language model, OpenAI. We'll

116
00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:35.876
import
a retriever called the self-query retriever, and

117
00:06:35.876 --> 00:06:38.785
then we'll import attribute info,
which is where we

118
00:06:38.785 --> 00:06:41.370
can specify different fields
in the metadata and

119
00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:42.663
what they correspond to.


120
00:06:44.798 --> 00:06:47.522
We only have two fields in the metadata,
source and page.

121
00:06:48.758 --> 00:06:51.699
We fill out a description of the name,
the description, and the type for

122
00:06:51.699 --> 00:06:52.540
each of these attributes.

123
00:06:53.318 --> 00:06:55.145
This information is actually
going to be passed

124
00:06:55.145 --> 00:06:57.428
to the language model, so it's
important to make it

125
00:06:57.428 --> 00:06:58.342
as descriptive as possible.

126
00:07:00.538 --> 00:07:02.981
We'll then specify some
information about what's actually

127
00:07:02.981 --> 00:07:04.202
in this document store.

128
00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:09.588
We'll initialize the language model, and
then we'll initialize the self-query retriever using

129
00:07:09.588 --> 00:07:13.915
the "from_llm" method and passing in
the language model, the underlying vector

130
00:07:13.915 --> 00:07:16.439
database
that we're going to query, the

131
00:07:16.439 --> 00:07:19.324
information about the description
and the metadata, and

132
00:07:19.324 --> 00:07:21.487
then we'll also pass
in "verbose=True".

133
00:07:21.487 --> 00:07:21.487


134
00:07:23.243 --> 00:07:25.785
Setting "verbose=True" will
let us see what's going

135
00:07:25.785 --> 00:07:28.327
on underneath the hood
when the LLM infers

136
00:07:28.327 --> 00:07:30.869
the query that should
be passed along with

137
00:07:30.869 --> 00:07:31.822
any metadata filters.

138
00:07:33.725 --> 00:07:36.754
When we run the self-query
retriever with this question, we

139
00:07:36.754 --> 00:07:40.692
can see, thanks to "verbose=True", that
we're printing out what's going on under

140
00:07:40.692 --> 00:07:41.298
the hood.

141
00:07:41.298 --> 00:07:44.402
We get a query of regression,
this is the semantic bit,

142
00:07:44.402 --> 00:07:47.789
and then we get a filter where we
have a comparator of

143
00:07:47.789 --> 00:07:50.047
equals between the source
attribute and a value

144
00:07:50.047 --> 00:07:53.151
of docs and then this path,
which is the path to

145
00:07:53.151 --> 00:07:54.562
the third machine learning lecture.


146
00:07:55.658 --> 00:07:58.821
So this is basically
telling us to do a lookup in the

147
00:07:58.821 --> 00:08:01.983
semantic space on regression
and then do a filter where we only

148
00:08:01.983 --> 00:08:04.883
look at documents that
have a source value of this value.

149
00:08:05.439 --> 00:08:07.373
And so if we
loop over the documents

150
00:08:07.373 --> 00:08:09.791
and print out the metadata,
we should see that they're

151
00:08:09.791 --> 00:08:10.999
all from this third lecture.

152
00:08:14.539 --> 00:08:17.716
And indeed they are.
So this is an example where the self-query

153
00:08:17.716 --> 00:08:20.100
retriever can be used to
filter exactly on metadata.

154
00:08:21.201 --> 00:08:23.400
A final retrieval technique
that we can talk

155
00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:24.499
about is contextual compression.

156
00:08:25.398 --> 00:08:28.347
And so let's
load in some relevant modules here, the

157
00:08:28.347 --> 00:08:31.002
contextual compression retriever
and then an LLM chain extractor.

158
00:08:31.002 --> 00:08:31.002


159
00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:34.669
And what this is going to do is
this is going to

160
00:08:34.669 --> 00:08:36.756
extract only the relevant
bits from each document

161
00:08:36.756 --> 00:08:39.103
and then pass those
as the final return response.

162
00:08:40.038 --> 00:08:42.061
We'll define a nice little
function to pretty print

163
00:08:42.061 --> 00:08:44.084
out docs because they're often
long and confusing and

164
00:08:44.084 --> 00:08:46.332
this will make it
easier to see what's going on.

165
00:08:46.332 --> 00:08:46.332


166
00:08:47.402 --> 00:08:51.017
We can then create a compressor
with the LLM chain extractor and

167
00:08:51.017 --> 00:08:53.729
then we can create the contextual
compression retriever passing

168
00:08:53.729 --> 00:08:57.344
in the compressor and then the
base retriever of the vector store.

169
00:08:57.344 --> 00:08:57.344


170
00:08:58.381 --> 00:09:01.202
When we now pass in the question,
what did they say

171
00:09:01.202 --> 00:09:03.510
about MATLAB and we
look at the compressed docs,

172
00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:07.100
if we look at the documents we get
back, we can see two things.

173
00:09:07.703 --> 00:09:10.726
One, they are a lot
shorter than the normal documents.

174
00:09:10.726 --> 00:09:14.051
But two, we've still got some of
this repeated stuff going

175
00:09:14.051 --> 00:09:17.074
on and this is
because under the hood we're using

176
00:09:17.074 --> 00:09:18.283
the semantic search algorithm.


177
00:09:19.603 --> 00:09:22.679
This is what we solved by using MMR
from earlier in this lesson.

178
00:09:24.241 --> 00:09:27.480
This is a good example of where you
can combine various techniques

179
00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:29.099
to get the best possible results.

180
00:09:31.624 --> 00:09:34.662
In order to do that, when we're
creating the retriever from

181
00:09:34.662 --> 00:09:37.699
the vector database, we
can set the search type to MMR.

182
00:09:39.282 --> 00:09:42.338
We can then rerun this and see that
we get back a

183
00:09:42.338 --> 00:09:44.375
filtered set of results
that do not contain

184
00:09:44.375 --> 00:09:45.139
any duplicate information.

185
00:09:47.462 --> 00:09:49.959
So far, all the additional
retrieval techniques that we've

186
00:09:49.959 --> 00:09:52.179
mentioned build on top
